Merge every child block of a block in one pass.

`optimizeBlock` merged one child block at a time: it built the parent's whole
list anew around that child, set it -- into the arena, which frees nothing --
and started over from the first child. A block with k children to merge in a
list of n costs n·k in time and in memory, and the children to merge are many
where Binaryen reads a stack the way a compiler of a stack language writes it.
A value pushed, then a local set, then the value consumed, is read as a block
in the operand of what consumes it, with a scratch local; `merge-blocks` lifts
that block out to a statement and then merges it into the function's body.
A Java compiler's `aastore` is that shape -- the index pushed, the value, the
value's local set before the index's -- and a resource bundle's
`getContents()` holds thousands of them: on one program of 420 000 functions
`--merge-blocks` alone peaked at 29 GB, and `wasm-ctor-eval` runs it after
evaluating.

Now one pass over the list merges every child that can be merged, building the
new list once and setting it at the end. The items a merge brings in can be
blocks to merge in turn, as before, so the pass is repeated while it merges,
which is as many times as blocks are nested, not as there are children. What
is merged, and what is dropped, is what it was: every decision reads the child
alone, and a merged item is dropped when it is concrete and not last of the
final list, which is also the last list the old loop set. Binaryen's tests of
the pass print the same modules, and that program's module is written byte for
byte the same, at 6.4 GB.

